## Neural Band and Air Gestures integration (this fork)

The optional native Neural Band integration uses Air Gestures for band connection,
profiles, phone controls and handwriting. Band gestures, motion/handwriting events,
profile associations and drafts are processed between your band and phone. The
integration does not upload them to this fork's developers, GitHub or GitLab.
Delivering a draft to another app or assistant can send it to the providers
configured for that destination, as described elsewhere in this policy.

**Meta sign-in, ownership and firmware services**

Enrollment through Meta is optional: you may instead import a compatible owner
bundle you already hold. When you choose Meta sign-in, Meta receives the information
you enter on its sign-in pages and the normal connection/browser information.
The sign-in uses its own WebView cookie store, separate from Faceclaw's other
WebViews. The resulting Meta account session is retained on the phone, encrypted
with an Android Keystore key; private owner keys and account sessions are excluded
from Android backups. An exported owner bundle authorizes band control and must
be protected by you.

Claiming a band sends the signed-in account session token, band serial number,
device certificates, an app public key, nonces and ownership receipt/signature data
to Meta's hardware service at graph.facebook-hardware.com. This happens as part
of the enrollment flow, after the user starts and confirms claiming. Claiming can
change the band's owner and unlink its previous controller. This integration is
not an official Meta service, and does not assume that a user-provided API key has
already established consent: the account sign-in and ownership confirmation are
separate user choices.

The optional Meta firmware tools can send the account session token, band serial,
firmware/version information and update-session identifiers to Meta's firmware
services, and download packages from the URLs Meta supplies. Downloading and
installing firmware are separate from updating this Android app. Importing an
owner bundle or using local band navigation does not require giving this fork's
developers your Meta credentials or keys.

Meta controls its services and their retention practices. Its policies are at:
  https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/
  https://www.meta.com/legal/privacy-policy/

**Optional Android access and phone features**

Air Gestures' accessibility features require Android's explicit accessibility
access. Depending on the feature enabled, they can inspect the foreground app and
editable field, enter recognized text, or perform navigation/touch actions.
Notification access exposes notification content for the selected notification
features. Phone-call permissions support call-related actions. These permissions
are not required merely to use Faceclaw's on-phone preview.

Speech and assistant actions can pass text or control to Android's configured
speech engine or assistant app; those apps' own processing and policies apply.
If a Google service is selected, see https://policies.google.com/privacy.
Air Gestures automation broadcasts can be received by other installed automation
apps. Its existing profile-announcement receiver can accept profile context from
other local apps. Native glasses input itself uses an in-process band listener,
not an exported automation receiver or the watch/TCP input interface.

**Unified app updates and development automation**

When the embedded update checker runs, it requests release information from
api.github.com for this fork and stores release/version information locally.
The request does not contain your Meta account token, owner key, band serial,
gesture history or draft text. GitHub receives normal request metadata such as
the connecting IP address and User-Agent. Following an update link opens GitHub's
release/download pages, which are subject to GitHub's policies:
  https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement

The repository's source-update automation runs on GitHub Actions, not on your
phone. It checks public GitHub and GitLab release feeds, fetches public source,
and prepares review branches and build artifacts. It does not read your phone's
app storage, band keys or Meta session. GitLab's policy is at:
  https://about.gitlab.com/privacy/

Issues, pull requests, screenshots and logs voluntarily posted to this public
repository are public. Do not include account tokens, owner bundles, private
keys, personal messages or unredacted device/account identifiers in reports.


## Wear OS Watch

The optional Faceclaw watch app (wear/) talks to the phone over Google Play
services' Wearable Data Layer, the same channel the watch uses for
notifications and other companion apps. Gestures, commands, the watch's own
speech-recognition results, and the mirrored glasses status and assistant
replies travel between your watch and your phone over their Bluetooth (or,
when paired that way, Wi-Fi) link; Faceclaw does not send any of it to a
server. Speech recognized on the watch uses the watch's system speech
recognizer, which may send audio to Google under Google's privacy policy:

  Google: https://policies.google.com/privacy

The phone does nothing watch-related on phones without Google Play services.
